---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CaseConverter from '@/components/tools/text/CaseConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="case-converter">
  <CaseConverter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert text case</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste your text, one line or a thousand. The case converter handles each line separately, so a list stays a list.</li>
      <li>Look at the preview grid: each style shows what your first line becomes.</li>
      <li>Press Copy on a style to copy the full converted text, or select a style to see the whole result and download it.</li>
      <li>Use “Use as input” to chain conversions. For example, make everything lowercase first, then apply Title Case for a consistent headline.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The thirteen cases, and when each one is right</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Style</th>
            <th>Example</th>
            <th>Use it for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>UPPER CASE</td><td>USER PROFILE SETTINGS</td><td>Warnings, short labels, legal headings</td></tr>
          <tr><td>lower case</td><td>user profile settings</td><td>Normalising input, email addresses, tags</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Title Case</td><td>The Art of the Deal</td><td>Headlines and page titles in editorial style</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Capitalised Each Word</td><td>The Art Of The Deal</td><td>Names, menu labels, table headers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sentence case</td><td>User profile settings</td><td>UI copy, buttons, modern documentation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>camelCase</td><td>userProfileSettings</td><td>JavaScript, Java and Swift variables</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PascalCase</td><td>UserProfileSettings</td><td>Class names, React components, C# members</td></tr>
          <tr><td>snake_case</td><td>user_profile_settings</td><td>Python, Ruby, SQL columns</td></tr>
          <tr><td>kebab-case</td><td>user-profile-settings</td><td>URLs, CSS classes, file names</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CONSTANT_CASE</td><td>USER_PROFILE_SETTINGS</td><td>Environment variables, enum members</td></tr>
          <tr><td>dot.case</td><td>user.profile.settings</td><td>Config keys, namespaces, i18n paths</td></tr>
          <tr><td>aLtErNaTiNg</td><td>uSeR pRoFiLe</td><td>Sarcasm on the internet, nothing else</td></tr>
          <tr><td>iNVERSE cASE</td><td>uSER pROFILE</td><td>Repairing text typed with Caps Lock on</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How Title Case is decided</h2>
    <p>
      Title case is not “capitalise every word”. The convention of AP, Chicago and most house styles:
      capitalise the first and last word and all other words, but keep articles (<em>a, an, the</em>),
      coordinating conjunctions (<em>and, but, or, nor</em>) and short prepositions (<em>at, by, for,
      in, of, on, to, with</em>) lowercase in the middle. This converter follows that rule. It always
      capitalises the word after a colon, dash or question mark, capitalises both halves of a
      hyphenated compound, and keeps all-caps acronyms such as NASA, API or PDF as you typed them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sentence case is the opposite approach and is now the norm in software: capitalise only the
      first word and proper nouns. It is easier to read at small sizes and easier to translate, and the
      design systems of Apple, Google and GOV.UK all specify it. The converter makes all text lowercase,
      capitalises the first letter after each sentence-ending punctuation mark or line break, and
      restores the pronoun “I”.
    </p>

    <h2>Identifier cases and how words are detected</h2>
    <p>
      To convert to camelCase or snake_case, the text must first be split into words. The splitter
      breaks on spaces, hyphens, underscores and dots. It breaks between a lowercase letter and a
      capital after it (<code>userName</code> → <code>user name</code>), handles runs of capitals
      before a word (<code>HTTPResponse</code> → <code>http response</code>), and separates letters
      from digits (<code>address2</code> → <code>address 2</code>). You can paste an identifier in any
      convention and convert it to any other.
    </p>

    <h2>Practical uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Fixing accidental Caps Lock: one click of iNVERSE cASE restores a whole paragraph.</li>
      <li>Renaming code across conventions: paste a column of field names and get the snake_case or camelCase versions at once.</li>
      <li>Cleaning imported data: spreadsheet exports full of SHOUTING NAMES get correct capitals.</li>
      <li>Headline consistency: apply the same title rules across a whole content calendar.</li>
      <li>Environment variables: turn a settings list into CONSTANT_CASE keys for a <code>.env</code> file.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Nothing leaves your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion runs locally, so internal field names, unreleased headlines and customer data are
      not uploaded. Large inputs are processed in chunks, and the interface stays responsive between
      them, so a file of several megabytes converts without freezing the tab.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
